22:28:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question—it really highlights how our perception of temperature goes way beyond the number on the thermostat. Even if the air temperature is held steady at 72°F (about 22°C) all year, there are several reasons it can feel noticeably cooler in winter: ### 1. Radiant Heat Loss to Cold Surfaces Your body doesn’t just lose heat to the air; it also radiates heat to surrounding surfaces. In winter, the walls, windows, and even the floor are much colder than in summer, even if the air is heated to 72°F. Since your skin is warmer than those cold surfaces, you lose more body heat through radiation, which makes you feel chilly. In summer, the surfaces are close to or above your skin temperature, so you radiate less heat and feel warmer at the same air temperature. ### 2. Drafts and Air Movement Winter often brings small drafts from windows, doors, or even electrical outlets. Even a gentle current of cooler air moving across your skin accelerates convective heat loss. Forced-air heating systems can also create air movement that feels cool, especially if the airflow isn’t perfectly mixed. In summer, still air at 72°F might feel perfectly comfortable, but add a slight draft and that same temperature can feel cold. ### 3. Lower Humidity Winter air is typically much drier, both outdoors and indoors (indoor heating drops relative humidity even further). Dry air encourages more evaporation from your skin, which has a cooling effect. Even though you’re not noticeably sweating, there’s always some insensible perspiration, and in dry air that evaporation happens faster, making you feel cooler. In summer, higher humidity slows evaporation, so 72°F can feel stuffy or warm rather than cool. ### 4. Thermostat Location ≠ Even Temperature Your thermostat only measures the temperature at its exact location, often in a central hallway away from exterior walls. In winter, rooms with large windows or poor insulation can be several degrees cooler than the thermostat’s reading, while still being part of the same heating zone. So you might be sitting in a 68°F corner while the thermostat happily reports 72°F. ### 5. Acclimatization and Clothing Your body adapts to seasonal temperatures. In winter, your circulatory system adjusts to conserve heat—blood vessels in the skin constrict, and your baseline comfort shifts toward craving warmth. You also tend to wear lighter clothing indoors year-round (short sleeves, light pants), so the same outfit that felt fine in July at 72°F can feel insufficient in January because your body is “tuned” to a colder environment. Exposed skin, especially hands and feet, easily feels cold due to reduced blood flow. ### 6. Psychological Expectation Finally, there’s a mental aspect. When it’s cold and gloomy outside, entering a room at 72°F doesn’t feel as warming because your senses are still carrying the memory of the cold. In summer, walking into the same 72°F feels like a refreshing escape from the heat, so your brain interprets it as cool and pleasant rather than cold. So while the thermostat works hard to keep the air at 72°F, your body is reacting to a whole thermal environment: radiant temperatures, humidity, air movement, and your own seasonal physiology. That’s why 72 in winter can send you reaching for a sweater, while 72 in summer feels just right.
